vaulthalla (1.14.0-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * upstream: provider administration for AWS S3 and Cloudflare R2 API keys
    (#237): capability probe, bucket inventory, managed bucket creation
    (collision-safe) and adoption (read-only admission check), Vaulthalla-owned
    lifecycle rule, detach, preflighted cleanup of buckets Vaulthalla created;
    `vh upstream …`, ws `upstream.*`, `storage.vault.add` `provision`, console
    credential/bucket/vault screens.
  * upstream: hard bucket protection locks keyed to (provider, account,
    bucket), enforced in every metered S3 request, the admin adapters and the
    ops; loaded at startup (fail closed).
  * sync: event-driven upstream sync (#96): AWS S3 → SQS Standard (queue
    policy, DLQ, notification GET-merge-PUT keeping foreign rules) and R2 →
    Cloudflare Queues (HTTP pull); durable deduplicated inbox written before
    ack, per-key ordering, self-write suppression, event-scoped sync passes,
    adoption baseline, bounded abortable long polls.
    `vh vault sync events --file` uses the same inbox.
  * sync: a provider removal event deletes a local copy unchanged since the
    last agreement instead of re-uploading it.
  * rbac: admin.upstream permission module (view, configure, provision,
    protect, unprotect, destroy); migration 110 adds it with the api_keys admin
    columns, upstream_bucket, upstream_audit and the event
    channel/subscription/inbox/intent tables.
  * fix: an object without Vaulthalla metadata overwriting a known file read as
    encrypted with the old IV and failed to sync; vaults that encrypt upstream
    still refuse it unless their event subscription imports foreign objects.
  * storage: new SigV4 signer (any service/region) and an HTTP client with
    connect/total timeouts and cancellation for the admin and queue APIs.
  * fix(shell): the CLI router's debug log no longer contains credential option
    values (`--secret`, `--password`, `--token`, …); they were logged verbatim
    when shell debug logging was enabled.
  * fix(vaults): changing an S3 vault's API key or bucket inserted a second
    binding row (`s3_pkey` violation), and creating a vault on a key + bucket
    another vault used moved that vault's binding to the new one
    (`upsert_s3_vault` conflicted on (api_key_id, bucket)); bindings are now
    updated per vault and a pair that belongs to another vault (live or pending
    deletion) is refused.
  * fix(storage): remote-only files indexed from objects that were plaintext
    from the start (an adopted or waivered bucket's data, another tool's
    upload) could not be read in vaults that encrypt upstream (the hydrate
    called them a downgrade); they are now sealed on the way in. A sealed file
    whose object turns plaintext is still refused.
  * upstream: a create-new attempt claims nothing until CreateBucket succeeded
    and the bucket listed empty (a transient HeadBucket/CreateBucket failure no
    longer leaves a "created by Vaulthalla" record that cleanup would trust);
    retries resume only a bucket the same record really created.
  * upstream: cleanup holds its own lease (adoption, create retries, detach and
    other cleanups are refused meanwhile; a lock or binding set during the run
    stops it), and a provider error part way is reported and audited as partial
    with the bucket left recoverable.
  * upstream: lifecycle writes rebuild only `vaulthalla-managed`; every other
    rule round-trips verbatim and is verified after the write; an externally
    customised `vaulthalla-managed` rule is refused rather than rewritten;
    re-enabling a disabled expiration needs the destructive confirmation.
  * vaults: deleting a vault on an adopted bucket refuses `--delete-upstream`
    (the retention purge refuses it too).
  * rbac: provisioning with a lifecycle rule or event subscription needs
    admin.upstream.configure; unprotect needs key consume; the upstream audit
    log shows only entries for keys the reader may view; repointing an
    administrative key (provider, endpoint or access key) turns administration
    off, and is refused while it operates event subscriptions.
  * fix(upstream): bucket names are validated before they reach a provider
    (vault create/update, every upstream op, the AWS/R2 adapters) and
    Cloudflare REST paths are percent-encoded; a name such as `x/../prod` could
    address another, protected bucket through the Cloudflare API, whose paths
    no signature covers. Legacy names can still be locked.
  * fix(upstream): bucket identity (and so protection locks) follows the
    credential's endpoint: an R2 or AWS endpoint saved under another provider
    label is treated as R2/AWS (unknown AWS account fails closed); new keys
    whose provider contradicts their endpoint are refused.
  * fix(upstream): provisioning regions are validated (AWS region codes, R2
    location hints) before they become host names or CreateBucket bodies, and
    the CreateBucket body is built as XML; queue policies refuse bucket names
    or account ids that would widen `aws:SourceArn`/`aws:SourceAccount`.
  * sync/events hardening (#96 review):
    - the index manifest's own notification is never applied or republished (it
      fed an endless PUT loop);
    - events are ordered to the millisecond (R2 has no sequencer); a
      create/remove tie is decided by one HEAD;
    - a removal is applied only after a HEAD confirms the object is gone (404);
      a failed HEAD is retried, never read as a deletion;
    - lost continuity (dead-letter messages, a poll gap longer than queue
      retention, events not stored before their last delivery) holds events and
      forces one full listing instead of trusting the index; DLQ depth is
      reported;
    - the applier takes the vault's pass lease before reading its batch, yields
      to a waiting regular pass, and stops between keys on shutdown; the
      service's own requests (teardown retries, HEADs, manifest publish) are
      cancellable;
    - provider rule changes of one bucket are serialized and every live
      subscription's rules are verified after a PUT;
    - write intents are removed when the request fails and live from
      completion; claiming one and settling the event are one transaction;
      unrouted rows get swept and are re-routed only within the subscription's
      prefix and age;
    - a scoped pass dropped before it runs releases its lease;
      `vault sync reconcile`/`inventory` take the lease;
    - `vh vault sync events --file` needs admin.upstream.configure +
      sync.config.edit + key Consume (it can delete);
    - vaults that encrypt upstream default to `foreign_objects=ignore` (adopted
      buckets keep `import`), and `vh-encrypted=false` over a file the vault
      holds sealed is refused unless the subscription imports foreign objects.

