vaulthalla (1.13.0-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * core: platform seams (core/include/platform,
    core/src/platform/{linux,darwin}) for the OS-specific syscalls; the Linux
    implementations are the previous inline code, moved. TPMKeyProvider is now
    crypto::secrets::KeyProvider over a platform sealer (Linux: TPM2/swtpm,
    unchanged blobs).
  * core: the kernel FUSE bridge is a build feature (-Dfuse, required on Linux
    as before); libfuse types no longer leak outside core/src/fuse.
  * build: Boost >= 1.89 (no boost_system library) and fmt >= 10 (CURLcode
    formatting) are supported.
  * fix: `vh <command> --help` could read an unset color theme (undefined
    behavior) while rendering help.
  * fix: the CLI's AF_UNIX address length is offsetof(sun_path)-based and
    over-long socket paths are refused.
  * fix: the unprivileged daemon no longer tries to run `usermod` when binding
    the first CLI user to `admin`.
  * tests: make test / make itest entry scripts are executable again
    (Permission denied on a fresh clone).
  * macOS (development only, not packaged): the daemon, vh and the unit tests
    build and pass natively on Apple Silicon (tools/macos,
    .github/workflows/macos.yml); FUSE is off and secrets use a development
    file backend.
  * fix(fuse): a rename with RENAME_EXCHANGE (or any flag besides
    RENAME_NOREPLACE) is refused with EINVAL; it used to be carried out as a
    plain rename that overwrote the entry the caller asked to swap.
  * macOS (development, not packaged): launchd LaunchDaemons and an idempotent
    provisioning script for the headless `_vaulthalla` system service
    (deploy/launchd); Linux installs exactly the same files as before.
    Provisioning refuses a conflicting existing account, symlinked or
    foreign-owned paths, and a purge into a mounted namespace.
  * vh: on macOS, the "not running", "not responding" and "permission denied"
    hints name launchctl, the log file and dseditgroup instead of systemctl,
    journalctl and usermod (Linux wording unchanged).
  * macOS (development, not packaged): HTTP uploads stage in a private
    directory under the state root when the build has no FUSE bridge, and the
    daemon refuses to start unless that state root is private to it (no
    symlink, no writable ancestor, owned by the daemon user). Linux keeps
    staging through the FUSE mount (`http_upload_staging=auto`).
  * build/dev: meson `-Dinstance=NAME` builds an isolated instance (own prefix,
    paths, account, units/LaunchDaemons, database, ports +100, `vh-NAME`);
    `make dev` now installs the `dev` instance beside production instead of
    over it, on Linux and macOS (`make dev-plan` prints the privileged steps,
    `make dev-uninstall` removes it). Production builds and `make install` are
    unchanged.
  * feat(cli): `vh fs ls|get|put` (#229): vault file access over the CLI
    socket's existing length-prefixed JSON frames (256 KiB base64 data frames,
    every frame bounded before allocation, at most 4 downloads and 2 uploads in
    flight); authorization through the HTTP lanes' evaluator
    (http::access::resolveUserPath, re-checked every 8 MiB and before commit);
    uploads staged privately and sealed by fs::Filesystem::createFile
    (compare-and-swap on overwrite); the client keeps local files local, writes
    outputs atomically and caps every reply frame (64 MiB, 512 KiB in a
    transfer).
  * feat(macos): installer package `vaulthalla-<version>-arm64.pkg`
    (tools/macos/pkg): self-contained (PostgreSQL 16 built from verified source
    against the OS only; every non-system library bundled into
    /usr/local/lib/vaulthalla; Node 24; libmagic's database via
    VH_MAGIC_DB_PATH); postinstall runs
    `vaulthalla-provision --apply --enroll <console user>`: a private cluster
    (sh.vaulthalla.postgres, _vaulthalla, peer socket in a 0700 dir, scram on
    127.0.0.1:36971), a generated role password handed to the daemon, the CLI
    admin bound to the installing user. Homebrew cask (vaulthalla/homebrew-tap)
    wraps the same package; release.yml builds it on GitHub-hosted Apple
    Silicon (signed and notarized with Developer ID secrets), attaches it to
    the GitHub release and updates the cask, without gating the APT release.
  * feat(macos): `vh setup caddy` / `vh teardown caddy`
    (deploy/launchd/vaulthalla-caddy, bash, no Python): Caddy as _vaulthalla
    under sh.vaulthalla.caddy (admin API off; unprivileged :80/:443), routes
    identical to `vh setup nginx`. Certificate source follows the name: an EC
    P-256 Vaulthalla CA with critical X.509 name constraints (the hostnames, or
    --ca-scope; replaced, never widened, for a name outside it), Let's Encrypt
    (HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN; --acme-staging), Cloudflare DNS-01 from a root-owned
    0600 certbot-format file, or --cert/--key. Generated config is validated as
    the service account before it replaces the working one.
    vaulthalla-provision keeps the daemon loaded once configured (so package
    upgrades restart it); the package bundles Caddy v2.11.7 +
    caddy-dns/cloudflare v0.2.4 (xcaddy, static). `vh setup caddy` on Linux
    points to `vh setup nginx` before asking for sudo.
  * build: zero warnings and no extraneous output on Linux (GCC) and macOS
    (clang), both with -Werror; tools/dev/quiet-build.sh fails CI on anything
    but progress (AGENTS.md). Third-party headers are system headers;
    tools/macos/env.sh treats CPATH as system includes and drops -L/-I entries
    for missing directories; libpqxx-vh is read through pkg-config (its
    `-l:libpq.so.5` made meson warn); test assets via fs.copyfile.
  * ci: the macOS workflow skips pushes to main that its merged PR run already
    tested (the Linux gate, parameterized); the CI container gets 20g (12g
    OOM-killed cc1plus); release publishes the macOS package to the Nexus raw
    repository (NEXUS_PKG_REPO, RELEASE_PUBLISH_MODE=nexus; identical bytes
    skipped, different bytes refused).
  * fix(macos): vaulthalla-provision reads dscl's native-attribute form
    (`dsAttrTypeNative:IsHidden`); a config.yaml still identical to the shipped
    template is rendered for the bundled database instead of kept.

